Summary: Don't release a freed OpenGL context on the main thread after it's deallocated on a GL thread
Category: graphics
Kind: fix
Origin: wine-mac-builds
Upstream: candidate (draft in .local/bug-reports/). The code is the same
 in WineHQ 10.0 to master and in CrossOver 26.3.0 (checked 2026-11-25).
Why: -[NSOpenGLContext dealloc] always calls -clearDrawable, which
 WineOpenGLContext overrides. Off the main thread the override runs [super
 clearDrawable] in a block sent to the main thread with OnMainThread, and
 the block retains self. When a GL thread drops the last reference (the
 context is usually freed on the main thread instead, when the block that
 removes it from its view is released), that happens during dealloc: the
 main thread runs the block, OnMainThread returns, the GL thread finishes
 freeing the context, and the main thread then releases the block and with
 it the freed context. objc_release reads freed memory and the process dies
 ("Exception c0000005 in system thread", at objc_release+34). The block now
 holds an unretained reference: OnMainThread waits for the block to run, so
 the context outlives its use.
 Also, -dealloc released the view and then [super dealloc] called
 -clearDrawable, which released it again whenever the context still had a
 view. -dealloc now clears the drawable first, so the view is released
 once.
 Native stacks of three caught crashes all ended in objc_release from this
 block's dispose helper, released on the main thread. A native probe
 (macOS 26) shows -[NSOpenGLContext dealloc] calling -clearDrawable with and
 without a view, and [super clearDrawable] neither retaining nor
 autoreleasing the context.
 Upstream winemac has the same code in 10.0; why 11.x shows the crash more
 often isn't known (Wine 11's wined3d may release contexts on its command
 thread, which has no event queue).
Programs: Nancy Drew games (Shadow Ranch, Old Clock, Crystal Skull, Castle
 Malloy) crashed at objc_release+34 ("Pattern A"); Steam's Nancy Drew games
 (Game.exe) crashed the same way 1 s after their first frame on 11.22; our
 d3d8-up-draw-speed test (wined3d on OpenGL) crashed in about 1 of 30 runs
 and failed CI's host GL tests
Seen on: macOS 26.7.2 (M1 Max): d3d8-up-draw-speed crashed 4 of 115 runs,
 gl-context-dealloc 5 of 5, on 11.22-build12
Expects before it: winemac-serialise-gl-context-changes (context)
Related: winemac-gl-update-on-main-thread; the same viewWillDraw and GL thread
 interplay
Status: 11.22: 2026-11-18: a winemac.so built with it, swapped into
 11.22-build12 (macOS 26.7.2): d3d8-up-draw-speed 200 of 200 runs passed,
 gl-context-dealloc (2000 devices a run) 5 of 5. 2026-11-25: in build0;
 gl-context-dealloc (1000 devices) passes on an 11.22 quick build.
 11.21, 10.0: 2026-11-18: ported from 11.22 unchanged; in the build0 test
 tarballs; no separate run.

diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_opengl.m b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_opengl.m
index 08a55f3..8b12d45 100644
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_opengl.m
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_opengl.m
@@ -78,7 +78,11 @@ - (instancetype) initWithCGLContextObj:(CGLContextObj)context
 
     - (void) dealloc
     {
-        [[self view] release];
+        /* -[NSOpenGLContext dealloc] calls -clearDrawable, which releases the
+           view this class retained; releasing it here as well released it
+           twice when the context still had a view. Clear it first, so that
+           call finds no view. */
+        [self clearDrawable];
         [latentView release];
         pthread_mutex_destroy(&changeLock);
         [super dealloc];
@@ -180,14 +184,28 @@ - (void) setView:(NSView*)newView
         [oldView release];
     }
 
+    - (void) wine_superClearDrawable
+    {
+        [super clearDrawable];
+    }
+
     - (void) clearDrawable
     {
         NSView* oldView = [self view];
         if ([NSThread isMainThread])
             [super clearDrawable];
-        else OnMainThread(^{
-            [super clearDrawable];
-        });
+        else
+        {
+            /* This runs from -dealloc too, on whichever thread released the
+               context last. A block that retained self would be released on
+               the main thread after OnMainThread returns, by which time the
+               context may be freed. OnMainThread waits for the block to run,
+               so an unretained reference (__block, without ARC) is enough. */
+            __block WineOpenGLContext* context = self;
+            OnMainThread(^{
+                [context wine_superClearDrawable];
+            });
+        }
         [oldView release];
 
         [self wine_updateBackingSize:NULL];
